Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Conclusions", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990744376878525*^9, 
  3.990744379202264*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d9274cb1-5e6b-4d07-a9d8-\
82dddcb2a8f7"],

Cell["\<\
Our thought-experiment of the gravitational-electrostatic equilibrium of two \
massive spheres reveals several fundamental physical and engineering limits:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990744384922296*^9, 
  3.9907444078008013`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"de28c1ac-8be5-494d-a6ec-\
f5b1f78e0451"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The Mass Limit of 1-Newton Gravity",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": As material density scales from steel to nuclear-degenerate matter, the \
sphere radius required to produce a 1-Newton gravitational force decreases \
from 2.95  m to 41.8 \[Mu] m. The required mass converges from above to the \
point-mass limit of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"1", "/", 
     SqrtBox["G"]}], "\[TildeTilde]", "122.4"}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"24e586c9-51c7-4bbe-a5ae-518a730494a3"],
 "  metric tonnes. All configurations remain many orders of magnitude larger \
than their Schwarzschild radii, avoiding black hole collapse."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990744384922296*^9, 3.990744493826643*^9}, {
  3.9907918397389393`*^9, 
  3.9907918403637037`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fad25e04-9714-4fc0-937d-\
c6269942f86a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Terrestrial Engineering Feasibility",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": While Osmium is mathematically the most compact stable terrestrial metal, \
its extreme rarity, high-pressure sintering requirements, and the lethal \
toxicity of osmium tetroxide make it commercially impossible. Gold is ruled \
out by astronomical asset costs and military-grade security requirements, \
while lead fails due to gravitational creep. Cast iron (steel scale) is the \
only realistic material, requiring a 20\[NoBreak]-\[NoBreak]foot diameter, a \
1,687\[NoBreak]-\[NoBreak]tonne load, and a project cost of \[TildeTilde]$750\
\[NonBreakingSpace] M using existing heavy-industrial foundries and shipyard \
logistics. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990744384922296*^9, 3.9907445944559603`*^9}, 
   3.990791848964252*^9},ExpressionUUID->"5d4f782b-3176-4b88-a712-\
c0428cdb34dd"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Electrostatic Stability and Quantum Limits",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": To balance gravitational attraction, the spheres must carry a \
charge-to-mass ratio of",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SqrtBox[
     RowBox[{"G", "/", 
      SubscriptBox["k", "e"]}]], "\[TildeTilde]", " ", 
    RowBox[{"8.62", " ", "\[Times]", " ", 
     SuperscriptBox["10", 
      RowBox[{"-", "11"}]], 
     RowBox[{"C", "/", "kg"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "f2bf9e67-5c4c-4ea2-a15c-5b41c628b6fd"],
 ". For metallic spheres, the resulting surface electric fields \
(\[TildeTilde]10\:2075  V/m) are stable in a vacuum. However, for degenerate \
matter, the microscopic radii compress the charge, generating fields \
(10\:2079 - 10\.b9\.b3  V/m) that trigger immediate Fowler-Nordheim quantum \
tunneling (field emission). Structural disintegration via Coulomb explosion \
is avoided, as the electrostatic pressure remains below the materials\
\[CloseCurlyQuote] bulk moduli."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990744384922296*^9, 
  3.9907447044216957`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"40688fff-e40d-43dd-bbee-\
f1def459113c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Mutual Polarization Divergence",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": When conducting spheres are placed near each other, like charges \
redistribute to the far hemispheres. This polarization reduces the repulsive \
force by up to 28.4% for metallic spheres at a 1-meter gap, requiring either \
a significant decrease in separation (e.g., decreasing the iron gap from 1  m \
to 0.005  m) or a \[TildeTilde]30% increase in charge. In contrast, \
degenerate spheres are so compact relative to the 1-meter separation that \
polarization effects are negligible, allowing them to behave as idealized \
point charges."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990744384922296*^9, 3.990744739033386*^9}, {
  3.9907925229711313`*^9, 
  3.9907925462863207`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2bf359b5-b6a4-4aa6-ac3d-\
fdff93accfe4"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["References", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990741960602479*^9, 
  3.9907419612213*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d50ee1be-35e2-4dca-8e29-087fe3e97ad8"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Field Electron Emission & The Fowler-Nordheim Equation", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9907419444068613`*^9, 3.990741969426816*^9}, 
   3.990742007955111*^9},ExpressionUUID->"c3561a97-7557-49e7-ae9c-\
023bfb7f91e0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Fowler, R. H., & Nordheim, L. (1928). \"",
 StyleBox["Electron Emission in Intense Electric Fields",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ".\" Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. Series A, 119(781), \
173-181. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9907419444068613`*^9, 
  3.990741986927363*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4d8a4cfa-d90b-4646-84d8-\
d1725b84d064"],

Cell["\<\
This foundational paper describes how high external electric fields warp the \
potential barrier at a metal/vacuum interface, enabling electrons to quantum \
tunnel out via what is now known as Fowler-Nordheim tunneling.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9907419444068613`*^9, 3.990741967102861*^9}, {
  3.990742098300033*^9, 
  3.990742098487705*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b4516ba6-9603-4a44-a0db-\
0515038b3da8"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Coulomb Explosion Effect", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.990741955211945*^9, {3.990741998502129*^9, 
   3.990742015191478*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7258eb69-4484-4000-9347-\
da1fa6991ef4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Schneider, R., & Rost, J. M. (1994). \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]",
 StyleBox["Coulomb explosion of intense-laser-produced plasmas",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ".\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] Physical\nReview A, 49(6), 5090."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990742296694016*^9, 
  3.990742330447504*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a8e40e44-d154-4063-82ca-\
180d4b9ae40c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Purnell, J., et al. (1994). \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]",
 StyleBox["Coulomb explosion of large gas clusters induced by intense \
femtosecond laser pulses",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ".\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]\nPhysical Review Letters, 72(13), 2030."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.990742296694016*^9, 
  3.9907423500921917`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a0a9796f-a18e-465f-882c-\
3caf24e64f34"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "While commonly studied in laser-plasma physics and cluster ionization, \
where rapid stripping of electrons leaves behind a highly concentrated, \
mutually repulsive positive charge core, the classical electrostatics of \
structural mechanical failure due to surface charge pressure ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"P", "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"1", "/", "2"}], ")"}], 
     SubscriptBox["\[CurlyEpsilon]", "o"], 
     SuperscriptBox["E", "2"]}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "f93b92da-ceed-4b18-b9b1-8c2e8102e85e"],
 " follows identical dynamic fragmentation principles. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.990741955211945*^9, {3.990741998502129*^9, 3.990742012718429*^9}, {
   3.990742053164342*^9, 3.990742094092956*^9}, 
   3.9907447484332457`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"935b8831-2111-4206-9ac1-\
7490c948bac0"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]]
},
